{"name": "round_landmark_interval_prior", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "", "hypothesis": "People do the same Bayesian concept learning over weighted mathematical rules and numeric ranges as the best model (size principle with tempered evidence, a decision gain and criterion turning belief into a yes/no answer), but the ranges they entertain are not all equally natural: ranges whose boundaries fall on round decade landmarks (such as 1-50, 20-29, 61-100 or \"the 80s\") get extra prior belief, by an inferred factor, over ranges with arbitrary edges like 23-87. Generalization from examples therefore tends to stop at, or extend out to, the nearest round decade boundary rather than tracking the examples' exact span.", "context": "experiment1 round 4 candidate 1 lens 2"}
{"name": "stochastic_example_forgetting", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "", "hypothesis": "People do the same Bayesian concept learning as the best model (weighted rules and all intervals of 1-100, size principle with tempered evidence, and a decision gain and criterion turning belief into a yes/no answer), but their memory of the examples is unreliable: by the time they judge a target, each example has been retained only with an inferred probability, and they infer the concept from whichever examples they still hold. Their belief is therefore an average over the possible remembered subsets of the set, so larger sets are sometimes judged as if from fewer examples (broader generalization) and an odd-one-out example is sometimes forgotten, letting the tight rule the other examples share (e.g. \"ends in 2\" after 92, 82, 27, 42) survive without being explained away as noise.", "context": "experiment1 round 4 candidate 2 lens 3"}
{"name": "criterion_shifted_redundant_examples", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "", "hypothesis": "Refinement of criterion_shifted_tempered_decision: people do the same Bayesian concept learning over the 42 weighted rules and all intervals of 1-100 (size principle, 10% noisy examples, tempered evidence) and turn their belief into a yes/no answer through the same decision gain and shifted (biased) criterion on its log-odds, but they treat additional examples as partly redundant rather than as independent draws. The one change, taken from redundant_examples_decision_gain, is that the total evidence from a set grows as an inferred power of the number of examples instead of in proportion to it, so with sublinear growth one example already supports a fairly tight concept while three or four sharpen generalization less than strong sampling predicts, while the criterion shift separately sets the overall lean toward \"yes\" (an exponent of one recovers the incumbent).", "context": "experiment1 round 4 candidate 3 refine incumbent criterion_shifted_tempered_decision"}
{"name": "split_rule_range_tempering", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "", "hypothesis": "Refinement of criterion_shifted_tempered_decision: people do the same Bayesian concept learning over the 42 weighted rules and all intervals of 1-100 (size principle, 10% noisy examples, tempered evidence) and answer through the same decision gain and shifted criterion, but they do not weigh the examples' evidence equally strongly for the two kinds of concept. The one change is that the size principle is tempered separately, by its own inferred weight, for mathematical rules and for numeric ranges: people may treat examples as strong, informative samples of an arithmetic rule (locking onto \"powers of 2\" after 16, 8, 64) while treating them as only weak evidence about where a magnitude range starts and stops (so range-based generalization stays broad), or vice versa; equal weights recover the incumbent.", "context": "experiment1 round 4 candidate 4 refine incumbent criterion_shifted_tempered_decision"}
